  "payload": {
    "rawResponseText": "Hotel owners in Europe who want **brand affiliation but more individuality** are often looking at **soft brands** and **lifestyle/collection brands** rather than highly standardized chains.[4][5][11][20]\n\nCommonly considered options include:\n\n- **Autograph Collection** by Marriott, which is designed for distinctive, independently styled hotels.[6][19]\n- **Vignette Collection** by IHG, which focuses on unique properties with community and local character.[6][8]\n- **MGallery** by Accor, which keeps each hotel singular in positioning and local market approach.[11]\n- **Emblems Collection** by Accor, positioned alongside Sofitel and MGallery as distinct rather than uniform brands.[11]\n- **Kimpton** by IHG, a lifestyle brand often associated with individuality and boutique-style character.[5][20]\n- **Canopy by Hilton**, which is cited as an example of collections of independently owned properties aligned by aesthetic.[20]\n- **Hotel Indigo** by IHG, which emphasizes local inspiration and individuality.[5]\n- **25hours Hotels** by Accor, described as individual and made-to-measure, with each hotel shaped by its location.[9]\n- **W Hotels**, **Moxy**, and similar lifestyle brands, which have been used as examples of major brands that still aim for boutique-style personality.[5]\n- **H+ Hotels** and **MAXX** within H World International / H-Hotels, which explicitly stress regional flair, local design inspiration, or individual hotel products.[1][3]\n\nIn Europe, these are often favored because they let an owner keep a hotel’s **local identity, design, and story** while still gaining distribution, loyalty-program access, and brand support.[4][11][20]",
